WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:10.170
Right now, somewhere in the universe, a giant
star is detonating, creating a huge cosmic

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.690
explosion called a supernova.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:25.020
Supernovas are a big, giant, dramatic end to
a star's life.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.910
All stars die, but only the biggest go out
with a bang.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.000
For a star to go supernova, we think it has
to be at least eight times more massive than

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:40.700
our sun.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.380
It's so easy to think of our sun as this
incredibly gigantic thing.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.630
But our son is absolutely tiny compared to
some of the giant stars in the sky.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.180
We can see some of these giant stars with the
naked eye.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:07.170
And the 10th brightest in the night sky is a
red supergiant around 15 times the mass of

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.450
the sun. Beetlejuice.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.380
Beetlejuice is so big that if you were to
place it in our own solar system, it would

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.020
stretch to the orbit of Jupiter.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.110
This is one of the biggest beasts in the
galaxy.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.770
It's a star also that is on the verge of
death.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:38.210
Beetlejuice is less than 10 million years
old, but this huge star's days are numbered.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.860
It's ready to blow.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:49.810
And when it does, we'll see a region of sky
brighten for 14 days until it's nearly as

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.850
bright as a full moon.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:56.870
It is going to be one of the most spectacular
shows in history.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.280
And it could happen at any moment.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.850
I mean, this is the thing. I often stand
outside in my yard in the wintertime.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.900
I look up at Orion and I see Beetlejuice and
I'm like, explode.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.960
So what will make Beetlejuice go Supernova?

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:20.000
To understand a giant star's death, we need
to understand its life.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.870
From the day it's born until the day it
dies.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.500
A star's life is a constant battle.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.970
Gravity is pulling in and energy pushing
out.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.330
The interior of a star is fusing countless
atomic nuclei together.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.980
Atoms are ramming into each other, getting
very, very close.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.270
And if they get close enough, they'll
actually stick and form a larger atom.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:57.500
Every second a giant star fuses seven and one
half billion tons of hydrogen.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:03.140
That amount of energy is roughly equivalent
to about 100 billion atomic bombs per second.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.970
That's a big ass explosion.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.570
This explosive energy threatens to blow the
star apart.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.790
But the star's own massive gravity keeps the
lid on.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.880
Everything in the universe is a fight between
the inward force of gravity and the outward

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.680
force of pressure or energy.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:28.040
Every single star in the sky.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.080
Even our own sun is an incredibly dynamic
battleground in many ways.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.060
Stars are an explosion that are actually too
big to explode.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.610
Gravity holds it together.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:45.690
This battle between these two opposing forces
determines the life and death of the star.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.610
And this is where size matters.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:55.240
The more massive the star, the more gravity
pushes inward and the harder the star has to

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.300
push outwards to keep itself alive.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.190
Very massive stars are like stars on
steroids.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.450
They have a lot of fuel to burn.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:12.610
They're so powerful that they use up their
fuel at a rapid rate.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:21.590
Massive stars like Beetlejuice are giant
factories fusing lighter elements into

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:22.610
heavier ones.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.930
But the hard work doesn't start until their
final years.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.480
For around 90% of their life, they fuse
hydrogen into helium.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.480
But eventually the hydrogen starts running
out.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.680
In the core of a Supergiant star.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.850
There's a sequence of fusion that goes from
lighter elements to heavier elements, and it

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.100
gets faster and faster every step of the
way.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.970
The countdown to death begins.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.270
The inward push from gravity takes over.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.490
Raising the temperature in the core.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.850
Helium starts fusing into carbon.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:07.590
There's enough helium to last about a
million years, but it too runs out and things

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.000
start speeding up.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.490
Carbon gets fused into neon.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.290
That takes about a thousand years.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.030
Neon fusing into silicon.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.920
That takes about one year.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:23.700
Once it starts fusing silicon into iron,
that takes one day it gets more and more

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.690
frantic. It's kind of like a cooking contest
show, whereas the clock is running down.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.930
They're trying to do more and more things
and they get more and more frantic until

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.760
Ding! Time's up.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.350
The star is now in its death throes.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:46.340
Once iron production has started, the clock
is ticking towards the cataclysmic end of

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.360
this star.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:56.490
A giant ball of incredibly dense iron forms
in the middle of the dying star's core.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:03.770
This iron sphere is several thousand miles
across and unbelievably hot.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:08.220
It gets so hot there, the temperature almost
becomes meaningless.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:12.840
I mean, we're talking about a billion
degrees in the center of one of these stars.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.620
This extreme heat is caused by fusion
reactions.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:27.300
More and more reactions create heavier and
heavier elements, and with each step, less

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.900
and less energy is produced until iron is
created.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.240
When you try to fuse iron nuclei together,
that takes energy.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.590
It doesn't generate energy.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:42.660
So once the core starts to fuse iron, it's
basically stealing its own energy.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:47.960
The growing iron core sucks more and more
energy from the star.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:54.350
Gravity continues, pulling in overwhelming
the outward pressure from inside the star.

00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:59.960
Everything gets crushed to unimaginable
degrees.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.820
All of a sudden, there's no nuclear reaction
to support the star against the crush of

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:05.510
gravity.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.570
With nothing left holding it up.

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:13.880
The star is doomed.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.840
Gravity winds the edges of the iron core
collapse.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:24.500
Trillions of tons of dense iron fall inward
at a quarter of the speed of light.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:28.820
The star has now less than one second left
to live.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:32.540
Things start to fall apart really quickly.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:37.940
The core collapse is so fast that the outer
layers of the star don't even have time to

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.440
react. They're just hanging there.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:43.250
It's kind of like Wile E Coyote when a cliff
collapses underneath him and he doesn't even

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.050
fall until he notices.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:55.080
The rest of the star collapses a trillion,
trillion, trillion tons of gas hurtle inwards

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.400
following the ion.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:01.800
Think about the entire mass of the star that
has been held up by nuclear reactions inside.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.290
All of a sudden those nuclear reactions go
away in a split second.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.170
Everything rushes into the middle.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.380
And that sets off the most dramatic
explosion in the universe.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.330
The spectacular death blow can outshine all
of the stars in the galaxy.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.680
But there's a problem.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.650
We still don't fully understand how a
collapsing ball of iron and tons of falling

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:39.190
gas create a giant fireball.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:45.670
How this collapsing core triggers a massive
explosion is one of the biggest mysteries in

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:46.600
astrophysics.

